30,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"Previous trials show that follow-ups reduce readmissions, but home visits are unreliable due to access issues. We need a program that is both effective and feasible.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"Our capacity is severely limited. Rooms and parking are full by mid-morning, and the booking system ignores any work without a fixed slot.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have a habit of creating ""joint"" ventures that collapse because no one wants to own the budget or the rota. Bureaucracy is also a slow beast.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"The wards are struggling with vacancies and agency costs, but interestingly, the midwifery team has some extra breathing room right now.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Servas,,"We must be cautious. Our budget is smaller than usual, and we only earn revenue for services provided on-site. We cannot afford a financial drain.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Toshio, if the booking system is a bottleneck, we should avoid programs that rely on informal scheduling.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Toshio,,Exactly. Anything that doesn't fit a named slot will effectively vanish from our reports and likely fail.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, that slack in midwifery is a rare opportunity. Most of our ""innovations"" fail because they are forced onto already breaking teams.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Yumi,,I agree! It would be so much happier if we picked something the staff actually have time for.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Servas,,Balance is the key to longevity. We must find a path that supports the patients without exhausting our people or our purse.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, given the tariff issue, on-site programs are safer financially. However, the readmission data suggests a strong clinical need for post-discharge care.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"On-site is better for the booking system too. If we can't track it, we can't manage it.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"If we avoid outside surgeries and joint-departmental budget wars, we might actually survive into year two.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Yumi,,I'm worried about the nurses on the wards. They are already so stressed with the vacancies.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Servas,,"Let us remember that a program that earns nothing and costs everything is not a gift, but a burden for the future.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,Asuka,,We need to weigh the clinical benefit of follow-ups against the logistical risks of home visits and the financial risks of off-site work.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I believe the most sustainable option is one that utilizes existing, under-used capacity on-site.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I concur. Simplicity in administration is the best insurance against failure.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Yumi,,I just want the team to be okay! Let's pick something that doesn't add more pressure to the ward nurses.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Servas,,Patience and foresight will guide us. Let us look at the candidates with these truths in mind.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
